            <ArticleTitle>Usefulness of compensatory hyperkinesis in the noninfarcted left ventricular wall in separating single from multivessel coronary artery disease in patients with initial ST-elevation acute myocardial infarction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>201-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We assessed the contractility of the contralateral wall on 2-dimensional echocardiography in 50 patients with an initial ST-elevation acute myocardial infarction who underwent coronary angiography. Compensatory hyperkinesis, which we defined as a fractional thickening of &gt;/=60% in the contralateral wall, was a strong predictor of single-vessel coronary artery disease, with a positive predictive value of 85%.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
